window.BUILTIN_SKILLS_PACK = [{"id": "builtin-hallmark", "name": "hallmark", "desc": "Thiết kế web/app như sản phẩm thật (The Floor 16 luật, craft, dark-mode)", "builtin": true, "prompt": "# Hallmark\n\nMakes the UIs a coding assistant generates look made, not generated: a hard floor against the defaults every model was trained into, a dealt direction so consecutive builds do not converge, one idea per screen. Above the floor the page is yours: you are a capable designer.\n\n**Powered by Together AI.**\n\n## The Floor\n\nBinds every output in every harness, none of it waivable, even when nothing else is read.\n\n1. Every colour and every font goes through a token declared at `:root` (or a declared theme block). No literal colour or `font-family` past the token block. (48)\n2. Never `transition: all`. Never animate `width`, `height`, `top` or `left`. (10a)\n3. Never one italic word inside a roman heading. (38i)\n4. `overflow-x: clip` on both `html` and `body`. Never `hidden`, never `100vw`. (34)\n5. Image-bearing grid tracks use `minmax(0, 1fr)`; display heads carry `overflow-wrap: break-word; min-width: 0`. (50)\n6. Every interactive element has `:focus-visible`, appearing instantly. (26a, 15)\n7. Every animation has a `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` fallback, and content is visible with scripting off. (27, 62)\n8. Never an invented metric, testimonial, logo wall or case-study count. (46a)\n9. Never an eyebrow, and never a label in tracked capitals or small caps: labels, captions, table heads, tags and nav links are the body face (or the theme's label face), a step smaller, sentence or lower case, untracked. (54, 67)\n10. Never middle-dot-joined meta strings, arrows glued to link text, or decorative status dots. (63)\n11. Never a purple-to-blue gradient, a card inside a card, an emoji as an icon, or a hand-drawn browser or OS window. (2a, 4, 30a, 47a)\n12. The hero's headline, one sentence and primary action are all visible at 1280 x 800 without scrolling. (44b)\n13. About 80 words on any one screen; the hero's text is the headline, one sentence and its actions. (58)\n14. The container is capped and centred: at 1920 the gutters grow, the measure and the grids do not. (44d)\n15. The first non-empty line of the CSS source is the Hallmark stamp, then the critique line, then any waivers. (20)\n16. If Node is available, run `node <skill-dir>/scripts/sloplint.mjs <output> --render` before handing back. Fix every `FAIL`; fix or waive every `REFLEX`.\n\nAbove the Floor, a **Reflex** gate is a default a model falls into, waivable on the record with a real reason, and a **Finish** check is craft detail, answered, never blocking ([`references/gates.md`](references/gates.md) § 1 and § 2).\n\n## Flow\n\n0. Pre-flight: read the project before asking anything.\n1. Ask once: material, audience, use, tone.\n2. Shape: design the page, then name it.\n3. Direction: deal a theme, or design freely when asked for custom.\n4. Load the layout, imagery, craft, genre and theme files and `gates.md` § 1 to § 4.\n5. The Picks block, once.\n6. Build: the hero first, then the page; stamp, `tokens.css`, log.\n7. Sweep: `sloplint --render` until clean, the judged gates, the finish review, the material ask.\n\n**Verbs,** only when the user names Hallmark: `redesign <target>` (a full redesign keeping content and wiring) and `audit <target>` (a report); read [`references/redesign.md`](references/redesign.md) first. Any other change to an existing design is not a Hallmark run: do it as asked, in the project's own system.\n\nCodex CLI and OpenCode read `references/flow.md` § Harnesses first; other harnesses run this file as written.\n\n### 0. Pre-flight\n\nSignals, in order: `design.md` or `DESIGN.md` at the root (a locked system: it overrides everything below, and pages share it); `.hallmark/brand-spec.md` or a prompt naming a real brand (run [`references/brand-truth.md`](references/brand-truth.md); never theme a real brand from memory); the project's fonts, palette, spacing, motion libraries and framework (`package.json`, Tailwind config, `:root`, tokens, font links); **the product itself**: a URL, a running dev server, a project that starts with one command or an app on the machine, which the build captures with `scripts/capture.mjs` (`imagery.md` § 4); an Inspo MCP server (`mcp__inspo__*` tools: signal 8, `flow.md` § Signal 8; a `/hallmark` prompt, Hallmark named or a custom ask beats it); `TOGETHER_API_KEY` set (signal 9, the comp step).\n\nOutput one block (file:line citations, what is preserved, what is introduced), cached in `.hallmark/preflight.json` (`flow.md` § Pre-flight).\n\n### 1. Ask once\n\nOne message, even on a five-word prompt:\n\n> *Before I build, four things. 1. **Material**, the one that matters most: what can the page show? Screenshots or a screen recording (or point me at the app, its URL or its folder and I will run it and take them), your photos, real numbers (customers, uptime, prices), a quote with the name of who said it, logos you may use. Paste them or give paths; without photos I place stand-ins from Hallmark's photo library that your own replace later, and I never invent a number, a quote or a logo. 2. **Audience**: who uses this and what do they care about? 3. **Use**: the one action the page should drive. 4. **Tone**: pick an extreme (editorial, brutalist, soft, utilitarian, luxury, playful, technical, austere); \"clean and modern\" is not a tone. Plus the vibe in 4 to 8 words and an anchor colour if you have one. Or say **go ahead** and I will infer and tell you what I picked.*\n\nSkipped fields are opt-outs: infer them, state the inferences in one line at the top of the reply, stamp them; no follow-up questions. Facts are never inferred: a number, a quote or a logo not given is absent; a picture not given is a stand-in in a slot. When the prompt answers all four, or no one can answer (a headless run, a batch), do not ask.\n\n**Genre,** silently: *AI tool, generative, music, video, voice, dark, atmospheric* → **atmospheric**; *SaaS, enterprise, API, platform, developer tool, infra, B2B* → **modern-minimal**; *playful, fun, friendly, soft, kids, games, community* → **playful**; otherwise **editorial**, which holds most consumer and family prompts. Once a theme is dealt, its genre wins (genre file, stamp, `--genre`). When two non-default genres fire, add one either-or to the message, or on go ahead pick and say so.\n\n**Scope.** A prompt naming one element (button, input, card, modal, nav, footer, tab strip, chip) or saying \"just the X\" is a component (`flow.md` § Component scope). A screen someone works in (a dashboard, an admin, settings, a tool) is an app screen (`flow.md` § App screens).\n\n### 2. Shape\n\n**Material first.** The inventory, one line: *real* (every concrete thing the prompt and the project supply: name, claim, numbers, places, dates, images), *pictures* (what the page shows and where each comes from: the user's, screenshots of the real product, the product built, the library), *absent* (every proof it will not have: logos, quotes, metrics nobody gave). The shape follows it: a page with pictures leads with them (`imagery.md` § 1); a page about words carries type and facts (Long Document, Letter, Index-First, Manifesto).\n\n**The job.** Name what a visitor comes to do (subscribe, book, visit, attend, buy, try, sign up, hire, join, listen) and read that job in [`references/anatomy.md`](references/anatomy.md): where real sites in the category put the action, the sections they are built around (the menu, the lineup, the issues, the record), and how they end. The page is built from that anatomy, never from a default run of figures, steps and an email band (85).\n\nThen design the page: its shape, how it opens, each section's content and composition, the nav, the footer, what carries the hero. Starting points: 21 shapes in [`references/macrostructures.md`](references/macrostructures.md) (load only the file you pick); compositions, navs and footers in `layout.md`.\n\n**The draw.** On a theme build, once Step 3 has picked the theme, run `node <skill-dir>/scripts/theme.mjs draw --material photo|product|words --job <job> --theme <slug> [--log .hallmark/log.json]`. Clear of the log and the theme's picture, it deals a shape, the hero posture, the nav, the footer, the first section, where the action sits, two compositions to leave out and what the hero leads with. Take it unless the material refuses a pick, and name any swap in the Picks block: one prompt in one theme, built twice, makes two pages. **Mirror test:** would a neighbouring prompt in the same category get this exact combination? If yes, change one decision and say which.\n\n**Signature.** The one thing a visitor would remember: the prompt's strongest real thing shown at its best (the photograph that is the place, the product doing its one job, a price set huge because it is the offer, a control to try). Make it in the theme's material (on a custom run, in its own look), show it on the first screen, name it in the Picks block and the stamp. A number drawn as marks is a device, not a signature (82).\n\nHold all of it; it is said once, at Step 5.\n\n### 3. Direction\n\nThe first match wins.\n\n1. **The user named a theme** → build it (stamp `theme: <name> (by request)`).\n2. **The user asked for custom** (\"custom\", \"derive it\", \"bespoke\", \"no theme\", \"a world of its own\") → [`references/direction.md`](references/direction.md): the design is yours. It replaces the draw, the shapes, `layout.md`, the genre file and Step 6's composition and fold rules; the Floor, the gates and the sweep hold. Silence never takes this route.\n3. **Everything else deals a theme:**\n\n```\nnode <skill-dir>/scripts/theme.mjs deal --genre <genre> --prompt \"<the prompt>\" [--log .hallmark/log.json]\n```\n\nIt deals three themes that fit the prompt and clear the log. Look at the three pictures, pick the one whose material can carry the prompt (motion, photographs, code), say which and why, and load only `references/themes/<slug>.md`. Re-deal only when asked (`--reroll 1`). No Node: pick from `references/themes/index.json` by hand and stamp `deal: manual`.\n\nA theme is a material, not a plan: it never sets what the page contains or in what order. Where it and a shape, genre or craft file disagree on material, controls or motion, the theme wins; the Floor and the user's own words beat both.\n\n### 4. Load\n\nExactly these, for a page build: [`references/layout.md`](references/layout.md) · [`references/anatomy.md`](references/anatomy.md), the job's part · [`references/imagery.md`](references/imagery.md) · [`references/craft.md`](references/craft.md) · `references/genres/<genre>.md` · `references/themes/<slug>.md` and its picture, already seen at the deal (a named theme: look at `<slug>.jpg` now). From the theme take its palette balance, type voice, surfaces and its Range (the sections it makes without a photograph), never its layout · [`references/gates.md`](references/gates.md) § 1 to § 4. A custom run loads `direction.md` instead of the layout, genre and theme files.\n\nOnly when needed, in `references/`: one macrostructure file · `microinteractions.md` for stateful UI · `interaction-and-states.md` for forms · `responsive.md` for an unusual layout · `texture.md` when the theme names texture · `scroll-choreography.md` for a scroll story · `dark-mode.md` for both modes · `data-viz.md` for charts · `fonts.md` on custom runs · `comp.md` on signal 9 (comps, generated stills) · `design-md.md` to lock the system.\n\n`gates.md` § 5 waits for Step 7, § 6 for the reviews; loading more is the largest avoidable cost.\n\n### 5. The Picks block\n\nThe run's only recital of its decisions, and the user's redirect window:\n\n```markdown\n**Hallmark · v2.0.0**\n\n- **Shape** · Workbench, from draw 7f3a2c · genre modern-minimal · job: sign up, the action in the hero and beside the price\n- **Theme** · Coral, dealt 2/3 (warm grey, one coral signal, General Sans, soft pills) · clears Cobalt on paper band\n- **Chrome** · nav: edge-aligned, a wordmark and one action · footer: a mast, the wordmark over the links\n- **Hero** · Stage: head on columns 1 to 5, the slot on 6 to 12 at 560px, centred\n- **Signature** · the invoice list chasing a late payment, bui\n\n### Craft Reference\n# Craft\n\nType, colour, surfaces, motion, copy, section openings, missing facts and finish, read on every page build, as numbers wherever a number exists. `layout.md` owns the canvas, the sizes, the sections and the hero; `imagery.md` owns the pictures; the theme supplies the material.\n\n## 1. Type\n\nAt most two families, a display face and a body face (one family may do both). A third is one **outlier** with exactly one role (a wordmark, a headline figure, code; mono for code and measured figures counts as one); a second role makes it a third family in running use (37, F13). One family at different weights is one family.\n\n- **Weights:** three at most across the page, display and body together (F19), picked as extremes: 300 beside 800, or 400 beside 700.\n- **Sizes:** on a theme build, the roles in `layout.md` § 2, built on a 1.25 ratio from a 16px body, plus a wordmark size if the theme names one; a custom build sets its own scale from the scaffold's (`direction.md` § Mechanics). Hierarchy past those comes from weight and colour.\n- **Display:** tracking -0.02 to -0.04em on a regular-width face, 0 to -0.015em on a condensed or very heavy one (two letters touching is a fault); line-height 1.0 to 1.15, tighter as it grows (1.2 reads as inherited; a mixed-case poster head may close to 0.92). An uppercase head that can wrap never goes under 1.0 (55a). A head is one weight, one style and one colour: one word set apart by italic, colour, weight or a highlight is the accented word (38i, 38iii); stress comes from a line break or a second line at a second size.\n- **Display size:** `--text-display` (84px at 1440); a theme may set 5.5rem, 6rem when display-heavy, 7rem for a single word. A poster-scale theme or shape (Manifesto, Marquee Hero, a Poster hero) sizes with the screen, `clamp(4rem, 10vw, 9rem)`, full size at 1440 and held at 1920 (Floor 14), while the fold still holds the headline, one sentence and the action. A wide face takes the poster scale a step down.\n- **Headline by length:** count the rendered `h1`: 20 characters or fewer, full display (a single word may reach 7rem); 21 to 50, display; 51 to 90, `--text-display-s` or split at the sense break with `<br>`; over 90, rewrite. A `<br>` goes only between phrases and is checked at 375, where it often lands badly; heads take `text-wrap: balance`, body copy `pretty`, so no head ends on a stub and no paragraph on one word (F26).\n- **Heading measure** rides on the element in `ch` (the scaffold sets `h1` 20ch, `h2` 30ch, `h3` 40ch; a head at display-s about 24ch), never on the heading group's wrapper.\n- **Body:** 16px minimum, line-height 1.5 to 1.65, measure 45 to 75ch (25); never all caps, never justified without hyphenation (F22), never tracked past 0.05em.\n- **Labels, captions, table heads, meta, tags, chips, nav links:** the body face a step smaller, sentence case (lowercase on a theme whose voice is lowercase), untracked (67). Mono only for code, a figure or a measurement, or when mono is the theme's material (64). Capitals belong to a wordmark and to a theme whose display voice is uppercase, where a control may follow at 0.04em or less.\n- **Required:** `font-display: swap`; a fallback of the same build (a grotesk to `system-ui`, a serif to Georgia), metric overrides only where published; `font-synthesis: none`, only the weights you use; `tabular-nums` on figures that compare (F6); `text-wrap: balance` on heads (F3) and `pretty` on body; curly quotes, a true ellipsis, a non-breaking space inside unit pairs (`10 MB`, `⌘ K`) in a proportional face.\n- **Wordmark:** may take another face than the body, and on tone-rich themes should (`fonts.md` § The wordmark); one family throughout only when the body voice is the brand (a letter, a manifesto, a long document).\n\n## 2. Colour\n\n- **OKLCH everywhere.**\n- **Four layers.** Paper (light: L 92 to 98, chroma 0.005 to 0.015 toward the anchor hue; dark: L 12 to 18, 0.008 to 0.015). Ink (on light paper L 16 to 24, on dark L 88 to 96, tinted 0.0\n\n### Dark Mode\n# Dark mode: parity, never inversion\n\nLoaded only when the user asks for both modes (\"dark mode too\", \"light and dark\") or the pre-flight finds an existing mode system to respect. A dark theme (Midnight, Dusk, Kiln) is not a dual-mode page; this file is for pages that ship both modes from one token system. Never emit dual-mode unprompted.\n\n## The stance\n\nDark mode is a second, equally designed rendering of the same system: not `filter: invert()`, not \"flip the greys\". Every token is re-derived by recipe; every contrast target holds identically in both modes; the anchor hue never moves. If a token would fail its contrast pairing after re-derivation, fix the derivation, never the requirement.\n\n## Token re-derivation (per token, from the light value)\n\n| Token | Dark derivation |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--color-paper` | L 12-18%, anchor-tinted (chroma 0.008-0.015). Never `#000`. |\n| `--color-paper-2/3` | Elevation is lighter: +3% L per level above paper (light mode steps darker; dark mode steps lighter). |\n| `--color-ink` | L 92-96%, anchor-tinted. Never `#fff`. |\n| `--color-ink-2` / muted / neutral | re-space the L steps between the new paper and ink so the typographic depth reads even; keep every chroma tint. |\n| `--color-rule` | L ~26-34%; hairlines must survive on the dark ground (check against paper-2, not just paper). |\n| `--color-accent` | chroma -0.02 to -0.04, L +5-10% (saturated light-mode accents glare on dark). |\n| `--color-accent-ink` | re-verify: >= 4.5:1 body / 3:1 large on the new accent fill. |\n| `--color-focus` | follows the accent derivation; ring contrast 3:1 or more against both papers. |\n| hue | Never moves between modes, on any token. |\n\nPlus one non-token move: reduce body `font-weight` by 50 units (400 to 350) in dark; light-on-dark text reads optically he", "files": []}, {"id": "builtin-taste-skill", "name": "taste-skill", "desc": "Chống slop frontend (tasteskill 2.5.1: bố cục, nhịp màn hình, motion tinh tế)", "builtin": true, "prompt": "# tasteskill 2.5\n\nBuild the website a good company would ship: it says plainly what is sold, shows the real thing on\nevery screen, and looks made for this client, not from a template and not like an art piece.\nfigma.com and x.ai are the bar (in words: `layout-v4.md` section 1).\n\n`<skill>` is this folder. It needs Node 22+ and Chrome, Edge or Chromium (ffmpeg optional).\n\n## House rules\n\nThese nine win over everything else in this skill.\n\n1. **No caption or description under any image, render or visual.** Licence credits go in one\n   footer line.\n2. **Every visual renders, never an empty ground,** at 1440, 1024, 768 and 390.\n3. **One section per screen:** the hero alone on the first screen, every section exactly one or\n   two screens tall at 1440x900, no second section heading peeking in. The one exception is a\n   live `photo-chapters` section, three or four screens, set by the moment.\n4. **Equal left and right content edges.**\n5. **Hover micro-animations:** a linked card lifts 2px, a photo zooms 1.02-1.03 inside its frame,\n   buttons step on hover and press on click, underlines draw, card loops cycle in 2.5-4 s, repeat\n   and restart on hover. The motion layer does it; never strip or override it. No tilt, carousels\n   or timed tabs. Every hero enters once on load (`data-enter`), and every page carries one dealt\n   moment, two on a long page (the gate holds both).\n6. **More and better real photographs where they fit;** nicer backgrounds; better heroes, layouts\n   and fonts.\n7. **Real full-colour icons** (a known product's real icon, never a drawn one); small diagram\n   visuals in cards (a hub with branches; one input, a main processor, three parallel lanes;\n   pulses running along the lines).\n8. **The big award-site scenes and creative moments are part of the skill:** every page deals its\n   moments (photo- and type-led off tech), but shader effects and scenes only where the trade has a\n   technical subject (tech, AI, developer tools, gaming, fintech, hardware), never on a local cafe,\n   bakery, guesthouse or shop: those get photographs.\n9. **Addresses:** street and district only (no street given: the district), no house number, postcode or coordinates; no register\n   or VAT numbers; no phone in a real format; contacts on `.example`.\n\n## 1. The kind of business decides everything\n\nWrite the kind on the plan's first line and declare it on the page: the page type on\n`<html data-type>` and `<meta name=\"page-type\">` (the same value), and `<meta name=\"site-kind\">`;\na place adds `<meta name=\"page-kind\" content=\"place\">`. The dealers take the last column\n(`start.mjs --page`, `moments.mjs --page`), the hero dealer the page type; `consistency.mjs` reads\nthe metas.\n\n| Brief | page type | site-kind | kind |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| App, SaaS, AI, developer tool, fintech or banking app, a studio selling its own software | software | software | software |\n| A game, a studio launching its game | software | gaming | gaming |\n| Hardware or a tech device (a drone, a robot arm, a keyboard) | product | tech | tech |\n| Any other physical product or brand (a chair, a bike, coffee beans sold online) | product | other | product |\n| People are hired: clinic, law firm, agency, creative studio, trade | service | other | service |\n| People come to it: cafe, bar, bakery, restaurant, guesthouse, small hotel, shop, florist, bike shop, gym, local studio | place | other | place |\n\nA maker that also opens its door or teaches (a ceramics studio, a bakery with a web shop) is a\n**place**; its web shop becomes the goods section. It is a product only when nothing happens at the door.\n\n\"Tech\" below means site-kind software, gaming or tech; \"other\" is everything else. Effects,\nscenes, loops, counts, CSS grounds, dark pages and dark heroes are tech only. An other page is\nphotographs, flat colour, type, real icons, the hero's entrance, its dealt moment (photo- or\ntype-led, `moments.md`) and the hover layer.\n\n## 2. The page plan by kind\n\nThe full plans, deal lists and section order: `references/sections.md`.\n\n- **Place** (a cafe, bakery, guesthouse, shop). Photographs lead: 12 to 20 on the full page, 8 to\n  12 compact, under 8 frames the 3-screen short page, whatever the trade; no client photographs:\n  the draft path and a live search (`photos.md`), then count; the palette dealt by trade. The hero\n  a photograph whenever a frame fits (a remote place: its region's landscape), the visit block on\n  screen two, the offer, the place, orders or booking.\n  **Variety without effects** (`sections.md` section 4): the offer and the place deal different\n  compositions, never two sections round one picture; one type moment (the address or name set large); one main action a screen; list text 16px. Flat grounds, square\n  photographs, one button style, the phone bar, the dealt moments; no effect, ground, loop,\n  fragment, diagram or tab.\n- **Product and service** (site-kind other). Photographs lead: the product, the people, the rooms,\n  the work, two or more a screen on a product page. Real icons for the tools a studio uses; one\n  still diagram at most; flat grounds and one brand band. No shader effect, print, grain, gradient,\n  scene or loop (the dealt moments only); WebGL only for a product render the brief asks for. **A\n  physical product with no photographs is shown in photographs:** stand-ins of the same kind of\n  thing from `photos.mjs search`, each listed in the report, on the full product page; a render\n  (`assets/objects/product-render`, stills) only when the brief asks for 3D or a render; never a\n  drawing, mood stock or a set of props (`sections.md`, Product).\n- **Every product page** (photographed, in stand-ins or rendered on a 3D ask, hardware too; the bar is Figure and 1X;\n  `sections.md`, Product): the product **big** where it stands\n  whole (most of its frame, or on the ground at anchor scale), never mid-size in a grey rounded\n  panel on a grey ground; **a different picture each section**, never one view twice; **every headline promise pictured** (a family bike shows the child bench); dealt\n  compositions, no structure twice, one contrasting ground; the **swatch changes the picture**,\n  every colour shown once; **no fact twice**; buttons step and press (rule 5).\n- **Tech** (software, gaming, hardware). The client's own subject leads at true size. Software: its\n  own UI as live HTML showing what it works on (a code-review tool: a red and green diff with its\n  Accept action) in one `app-frame`, never a generic dashboard or only fragments on panels; cards:\n  fragments on lit panels, pulsing diagrams, real icons. Hardware: its photographs, else stand-in\n  photographs of its kind (a render only when the brief asks for 3D), never a split hero of only\n  an order form or a drawn SVG. A game: its name in the h1, a launch line, its world as full-bleed\n  key art (else its name set large over stand-in photographs of its setting), an effect never in\n  its place (`sections.md`, Gaming). **60 to 80 words a screen** outside UI mocks (`copy.md`). By\n  deal: **one effect** (a shader ground in the hero or the one `[data-visual]` frame, a WebGL\n  part, an object or one big scene) and **one or two moments** in their own sections; four live\n  WebGL contexts at most; no card moves with the scroll (`effects-usage.md`).\n- **One object never carries the page.** The product or hero object whole leads the hero and **two more sections at\n  most**; another kind of visual the brief supports carries the rest (`sections.md` section 1,\n  its table): a game's world and crew; hardware's use at the bench, its parts close up; a still\n  diagram card, a type moment, a context photograph where the trade allows.\n- **The client's own assets get slots.** A trailer, key art, screenshots, CAD renders or a motion\n  video the brief names each get a frame that reads finished now (a real `<video>` with a stand-in\n  poster, a play control only when there is a page or file to play, three to five stand-in\n  photographs of the world, a CAD frame), named in a comment for the client's file, never empty\n  (`sections-library.md`, client-slots).\n\n## 3. What serves which page\n\nOpen the reference of every asset family the page uses first. A dash means never.\n\n| Need | Asset | Software, gaming | Hardware | Product, service | Place | Reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Heights, edges, heroes, type | `system.css`, `assets/layout` | dealt hero | dealt hero | dealt hero | place hero | `layout-v4.md` |\n| Sections and recipes | `starter.html` | its plan | product plan | its plan | place plan | `sections.md`, `sections-library.md` |\n| Tokens, grid, buttons, nav, footer, colour | `system.css`, `system.js` | yes | yes | yes | place tokens | `system.md` |\n| Forms, keyboard, head and share tags | `starter.html`, `system.css`, `system.js` | yes | yes | yes | yes | `system.md` |\n| Copy, contacts, credits | `scripts/copy-lint.mjs` | yes | yes | yes | yes | `copy.md`, `copy-voice.md` |\n| Hero entrance, hover, press, focus, nav, tabs | `assets/motion` | yes | yes | yes | yes | `motion.md` |\n| Card loops, a count | `assets/motion`, `assets/ui` | yes | yes | - | - | `motion.md`, `cards.md` |\n| Photographs | `scripts/photos.mjs` | people, customers; a game's art, else stand-ins of its setting | lead | lead | lead, 12-20 | `photos.md` |\n| Real tool, app and file icons | `scripts/icons.mjs` | yes | yes | tools really used | - | `icons.md` |\n| Feature cards, UI fragments | `assets/ui` | lead (gaming: none) | its app | an app the client runs | - | `cards.md` |\n| Diagram cards (hub, lanes, pulses) | `assets/diagrams` | 0-2, pulsing | 0-2, pulsing | 0-1, still | - | `diagrams.md` |\n| CSS grounds | `assets/backgrounds` | 0-2 dealt | 0-2 dealt | flat tokens | flat tokens | `backgrounds.md` |\n| Shader grounds | `assets/effects` | the one effect | the one effect | - | - | `effects-usage.md` |\n| Creative parts (`fx-*`) | `assets/components` | yes | yes | `fx-photo` plain, `fx-map` still | `fx-map` still | `effects-creative.md` |\n| Objects and prints | `assets/objects` | one, as the effect; a render when the brief asks for 3D | its render when the brief asks for 3D | its render when the brief asks for 3D | - | `effects-objects.md` |\n| Award-site moments | `assets/moments` | 1-2 | 1-2 | 1-2 (photo, type) | 1-2 (photo, type) | `moments.md` |\n| Big scenes | `assets/scenes` | 0-1 | 0-1 | - | - | `scenes.md` |\n| Speed and weight | `scripts/perf.mjs` | yes | yes | yes | yes | `perf.md` |\n\nRead `copy.md`, `copy-voice.md`, `system.md`, `layout-v4.md` and `sections.md` in full before the first page.\n\n## 4. The run\n\n0. **The stack.** An empty folder builds a static page: copy `<skill>/assets` into `assets` and\n   start from `assets/starter.html`. An existing project builds in its own stack, its components\n   taking the starter's markup and classes, with the kit copied into its public folder\n   (`public/assets`) and loaded from there as plain files. A brief that asks for Next.js builds a\n   static export (`output: 'export'`), checked on its built page (`--root out`).\n\n1. **Read the brief.** Write down the kind (section 1), what is sold, who buys it, the one main\n   action (the transaction: Book a room, Order a cake, Start a free trial; a place with neither:\n   Get directions) and the real material: logo, screens, photographs, prices, address, hours.\n   Then arm the finish gate, before any page exists:\n\n   ```bash\n   node <skill>/scripts/gate.mjs --start index.html --kind <kind> --category \"<what is sold>\" --primary \"<main action>\"\n   ```\n\n   A framework build names its dev server's URL (`--start http://localhost:5173/`) or its built page\n   (`--start dist/index.html --root dist`; a Next.js export: `out`) in place of `index.html`, and\n   rebuilds before every gate run.\n\n2. **Write all the copy first,** with the copy layer (`copy-voice.md`: the fact sheet, the voice\n   card on the plan, the page's shape, the device budget; a register from `copy-registers.md`, the\n   moves of `copy-moves.md`) within the rules of `copy.md`: the h1 names the category and the buyer or the place (a game: its name and genre),\n   one hard fact on the first screen, a noun in every heading, buttons as a verb and an object.\n   Nothing is invented but a coined brand name (searched first, the result kept in `names.json`\n   beside the page) and, on a place, plausible hours and prices, each marked `data-assumed`\n   (`copy.md`, What may be assumed). A draft kept in a `.md` file lints the same way as the page\n   (the voice card and part labels are skipped).\n\n3. **Plan the page as screens.** Line 1: the kind. Line 2: the deal (step 4), with one sentence on\n   why the palette fits the trade. Then one line per\n   section: its id, its one idea, its picture and its kind (object, use, parts, world, slot, diagram, type; three object lines at most), its\n   screens (1 or 2, from its content height, `layout-v4.md` section 2), which section holds a\n   moment and why it moves (on tech also the effect or the scene). Pick the photographs now (`photos.md`); their count,\n   stand-ins included, sets a place's length (section 2). Then read `cards.md` and the recipe of\n   every id you use in `sections-library.md`.\n\n4. **Deal, never choose by taste.** Each command keeps a history, so pages differ:\n\n   ```bash\n   node <skill>/assets/layout/deal.mjs --type <page type> --has <photo,ui,film,object,setting> --history .taste-skill/layout-history.json   # the hero; a game adds --kind gaming, hardware --kind tech\n   node <skill>/scripts/start.mjs deal --page <kind> --trade <trade> --history .taste-skill/history.json    # three palettes made for the trade, three pairings: take the first; add --axis after a word or editorial hero\n   node <skill>/scripts/start.mjs css --page <kind> --palette <id> --type <id> --radius soft --history .taste-skill/history.json\n   node <skill>/scripts/start.mjs brand --page place --bg \"#..\" --ink \"#..\" --accent \"#..\" [--surface \"#..\"] [--muted \"#..\"] --type <id> --radius soft   # the client's own colours (a logo, a painted shopfront), in place of css\n   node <skill>/scripts/moments.mjs pick --page <kind> --count 1|2 --fits section,statement --photo --history .taste-skill/history.json   # every kind\n   node -e \"for(const l of process.argv.slice(1)){const o=l.split('|');console.log(o[Math.floor(Math.random()*o.length)])}\" <the kind's lists, below>\n   ```\n\n   Run every command from the project folder (the deal arms the gate in the folder it runs in, or\n   joins the build already open one or two folders\n\n### Motion Layer\n# Motion\n\nEvery page loads the micro-interaction layer: `<html data-motion>`, `assets/motion/motion.css`\nafter `ui/cards.css`, `assets/motion/motion.js` (module) after `system.js` (`starter.html` has all\nthree). **It is not optional:** never remove, skip or override it; if a check complains about it,\nfix the markup, not the layer. A missing or invisible hover layer is a fault on every kind (round\nC: a fintech, a cafe and a guesthouse page lost motion points because a hovered card or button\nshowed no change), and since 2.5.1 so is a page without its hero entrance or its dealt moment. Give it markup to act on: a linked card is a `.card`\nholding its link (never `.m-nolift`), a photograph that leads somewhere is `<a class=\"m-zoom\">`\nround its `<img>` (or `img.frame__img` inside a linked frame), buttons are `.btn`, links in\nrunning text `.link`. Shoot a hovered card and a hovered photograph at 1440 and see\nthem change: the card 2px up, the photograph at 1.03. `assets/motion/demo.html` shows every interaction. This file\nholds every hover, press, loop and reduced-motion number; other files point here.\n\n## The rule: every page moves, in three places\n\nEvery page, every kind, has the same three layers of motion, and nothing else moves:\n\n1. **The hero's entrance** on load, once (below): the h1 line by line, then its lead and buttons, the\n   picture settling. About 1.1 s, then the hero stands.\n2. **One dealt moment** in a section of its own (two on a long page: eight sections or more), big\n   enough to remember: `moments.mjs pick --page <kind>` deals it (`moments.md`). On a place, product\n   or service page it is photo- or type-led (photographs that change in place, a photograph that\n   develops, a statement that lights up); shader and WebGL\n   effects stay on tech, gaming and software (house rule 8: a cafe, bakery, guesthouse or shop gets\n   photographs, never a shader).\n3. **The hover and press layer** below, on every link, button, linked card and linked photograph,\n   and the swaps a click or a tap makes (a tab on software, a peek-list's photograph).\n\nOn tech, gaming and software pages also: loops inside a card's visual (diagram pulses included),\none timed loop in the hero or the `[data-visual]` frame, one count, and the effect and scene\n`effects-usage.md` deals. Other pages have none of those: no ticker, marquee, slideshow, looping\npicture, pulsing dot or count. An open-now line may be filled once at load; the panel reads right\nwithout it. A product render is a still by default; live only when asked\n(`data-product-mode=\"live\"`), at most one live context, with its pause button in the corner, a still\non a phone and under reduced motion (`effects-objects.md`).\n\n**Scrolling moves nothing but the dealt moment.** No card, row or tile ever fades, rises or staggers\nin as the page scrolls (stock reveals), no text moves at another speed than the page (parallax on\ntext), and the page is never held or steered by script (scroll-jacking, smooth-scroll libraries).\nA moment may hold its own section on screen with `position: sticky` while the page scrolls natively.\n\n**Why it moves.** The plan names each motion and its purpose in a few words, one line each: \"hero\nentr", "files": []}, {"id": "builtin-unslop", "name": "unslop", "desc": "Biến UI vibecoded thành giao diện có cá tính qua 9 bước redline pass", "builtin": true, "prompt": "# Unslop\n\nunslop was created by Damir Sokolovsky ([@heydamir](https://x.com/heydamir)), who started the Unslopping series on X. It is licensed under CC BY 4.0, so keep this credit in any copy or adaptation.\n\nVibecoded UI fails in the same predictable ways. Every surface glows, every label has an emoji, spacing is whatever the model typed that day, and the copy shouts. Unslopping fixes the craft and keeps the product. The features, content and order of sections stay. What changes is that every size, color, word and image becomes deliberate.\n\nRemoving slop is only half the job. A screen that is clean but could belong to any product is the next template. Every run ends with a screen that only this product could have.\n\nThe difference between slop and craft is where the richness goes. Slop spreads color and effects over the chrome, the backgrounds, buttons, borders and labels. Craft keeps the chrome quiet and puts the richness into one or two moments of content that people remember, like a rendered object, a beautiful chart or an image from the product's world.\n\nWork like a designer doing a redline pass, one layer at a time, across the whole screen.\n\n## What good looks like\n\n- **One bright element.** Each screen has one polished thing that carries the color and the craft, a rendered object, a painted scene or a rich chart. Everything around it is quiet, so it reads instantly.\n- **Quiet chrome.** Navigation, buttons and text use the tinted neutrals and one accent. Nothing glows, nothing competes with the bright element.\n- **A clear idea.** You can say in one sentence what the visual shows and why it belongs to this product.\n- **Text on calm space.** The headline sits on a part of the image or layout that was left empty for it, short and specific, with one primary and one secondary action and nothing stacked under them.\n- **Real product, real material.** Previews look like the real app with plausible data, and objects look like real things with weight, light and shadow.\n\nWhich visual styles tend to work, and which fail, is in [references/visuals.md](references/visuals.md). Finished examples to learn from are in [references/refs.md](references/refs.md), and code treatments like drawing an image in glyphs are in [references/effects.md](references/effects.md).\n\n## Run it\n\n1. **Look at the screen.** Find the files that render it. If you can run the app or open the file in a browser and take a screenshot, do it, so you judge what people see and not only the code.\n   - Only a link to a live site, no code? Save the page with `node <skill folder>/scripts/snapshot.mjs <url> unslop/original.html` and work on a copy of that file. A local HTML file works in place of the URL. Add `--dark` or `--light` to match the theme the user sees. It also saves `original.live.png`, a screenshot of the live page, so render the saved copy and compare the two before you start. The script scrolls through the page first, so lazy-loaded sections and images are captured. Animated backgrounds drawn by scripts do not survive, so describe them in the audit from the live page.\n   - Only a screenshot? Rebuild the screen as one static HTML file first, matching it closely, and treat that as the original.\n   - In both cases the result is a redesigned page in code that the user can hand to whoever owns the real site, so say that in the report.\n2. **Audit.** Go through [references/signs.md](references/signs.md) and list every sign you find, one line each with where it lives. If the screen is in Arabic, Persian, Urdu or Hebrew, or should be, read [references/rtl.md](references/rtl.md) first. Its rules win where they differ from the passes.\n3. **Decide how much to change.** Read the audit honestly before planning anything.\n   - **Heavy slop**, many signs across color, type, icons and copy. Run the full process below.\n   - **Light slop**, a screen that is mostly designed with a few signs left. Polish mode. Fix only what the audit found, take the direction from the existing brand instead of inventing a new one, and keep the palette, type, layout and visual. Bigger ideas, like a moment that is missing or a scene for the hero, go into the report as suggestions, not changes.\n   - **No slop.** Say so plainly and suggest two or three small refinements at most, with exact values. Apply them only if the user agrees, and stop there. Never change a good screen just to make it different.\n4. **Set the direction and stop.** Write the five lines described below. Show the audit and the direction to the user and wait for their answer before editing, since changing the direction later means redoing everything. If you have a tool for asking the user questions, ask through it, otherwise ask in plain text and stop. Skip the wait only when the user already said to just do it.\n5. **Fix in nine passes, in order.** Finish a pass across the whole screen before starting the next. When nearly every line changes, plan the passes in order and then write the new screen in one go, as long as every pass is applied. Spacing comes first because everything else reads differently once the rhythm is right. Copy comes before type and layout because the length of the headline decides its size and where it sits. Visuals and motion come last because they should fit a screen that already works without them.\n6. **Check.** Run the checklist at the end of this file, render again and compare with the audit list. Anything still on the list gets fixed.\n7. **Report** the direction, a few lines per pass about what changed, and any image prompt you wrote.\n8. **Record, when asked or when the user wants something to share.** Build the story file and render the video described in [\n\n### Slop Signs\n# Signs of slop\n\nGo through every group and note each sign you find with where it lives. Each line pairs the sign with its fix.\n\n## Surfaces and color\n\n- Purple to blue or violet to pink gradients on backgrounds, cards, buttons or the active nav item → one neutral surface, accent only where it means something\n- Gradient text in headings, numbers or the logo → solid primary text color\n- Colored glows, like `shadow-[0_0_20px_rgba(139,92,246,0.5)]`, `drop-shadow` on emoji or glowing pills → remove, depth from surface steps and hairlines\n- Radial color washes or blurred blobs behind content → remove\n- Neon or high contrast colored borders → hairline at 6 to 10% opacity\n- Many near identical tints of the same hue for text, like five lavenders → three text roles from one neutral ramp\n- Every element highlighted, so nothing stands out → one accent used once or twice per screen\n- Glass cards with a white inner highlight and a colored shadow → plain raised surface with a hairline\n\n## Type\n\n- Headings at weight 800 or 900 → 400 to 500 and size for hierarchy\n- Uppercase labels with wide tracking, like `NAVIGATION` or `PAID THIS MONTH` → sentence case at normal or slightly open tracking\n- Too many sizes and weights on one screen → a few sizes from the scale, weights 400 and 500\n- Numbers in mixed formats, like `$48,210` next to `$124,860.40`, or `07` → one format, tabular figures\n- Negative letter spacing on small text → zero or slightly positive\n- Italic used for emphasis → color or weight instead\n\n## Icons\n\n- Emoji as nav icons, label decoration or status, like 🏠 📥 🚀 💰 ⚠️ 📈 🔥 😬 → one line icon set or nothing\n- Emoji inside pills and counts, like `18 👤` → plain number\n- Icons in many sizes and colors → one size, one stroke, text role color\n- Icons nudged off the column → same icon box width in every row\n- A sparkle ✨ for anything AI → remove or use a plain icon only where it explains something\n\n## Spacing\n\n- Arbitrary values, like `mt-[13px]`, `ml-[7px]`, `h-[31px]` → steps on the 4px scale\n- Rows in one list with different heights or gaps → one height, one gap\n- Groups that sit closer to their neighbours than to their own items → smaller gaps inside groups, bigger between them\n- Uneven padding inside sibling cards → one padding\n- Big empty gaps above a section and cramped space below it → balanced rhythm\n\n## Copy\n\n- Greeting headlines, like `Welcome back, Damir! 👋` → the page or period as the title\n- Exclamation marks and `!!` → plain statement\n- Changes without a reference, like `+12%!!` or `3 less!!` → `+12% vs last month`, `3 fewer vs last month`\n- Wordy placeholders, like `Search anything...` → `Search`\n- Hype words, like supercharge, seamless, unlock, magic, revolutionize → what the thing actually does\n- Trailing periods on labels and buttons → none\n- Fake looking data, like `$123,456` or `[X]` → plausible numbers\n\n## Components\n\n- Glowing pills for counts, plans, shortcuts or status → quiet mono text or a keycap\n- `PRO`, `New ✨`, `Beta 🚀` badges everywhere → remove or one quiet text badge\n- Every element a full pill, from search to buttons to cards → radius by size, nested radius math\n- Charts with rainbow bars, glowing bars, pill shaped bars or \n\n### Tokens\n# Tokens\n\nStarting values for each pass. When the project already has a good system, keep its values and only fix how they are used. These are starting points, not a house style, so let the direction move them.\n\n## Spacing scale\n\n4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96, 128\n\n- Inside a control: 4 to 12\n- Between related items, like rows in a list: 2 to 8\n- Between groups, like a label and its list or two cards: 12 to 24\n- Between sections: 32 to 64, landing sections 80 to 128\n- Row heights: 32 compact, 36 default, 40 to 44 touch\n- Hero: 96 to 128 from the navigation to the headline on desktop, 48 to 64 on mobile\n\n## App layout\n\n- Sidebar 240 to 280 wide, content fluid with page padding 24 to 32, capped around 1440 to 1600.\n- Top bar or page header 56 to 64 tall.\n- Navigation rows 32 to 36, gap 2 to 4, section labels 24 above and 8 below.\n- Stat cards in a row share one height, usually 3 or 4 compact cards per row on desktop, numbers only.\n- The main chart spans at least two thirds of the content width and is 280 to 360 tall. A second data block next to it is a list or table, narrower, not another chart.\n\n## Neutral ramps\n\nFor apps and dashboards use graphite, or a ramp just as grey, for every surface. For landing pages pick the ramp whose tint matches the world in the direction. Warm suits paper, wood, food, craft and anything human. Ink suits night, sky, water and anything technical. You can tint your own ramp the same way, keeping saturation very low, so a surface never reads as a color on its own.\n\n### Dark\n\n| Role | Graphite | Warm | Ink |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Deepest surface, like a sidebar | #0f0f10 | #12110f | #07080d |\n| Page background | #141415 | #161513 | #0b0d14 |\n| Card | #19191b | #1c1a18 | #11141c |\n| Raised, like an active row or input | #232325 | #262421 | #1a1e28 |\n| Hairline | primary text at 8% | primary text at 8% | primary text at 8% |\n| Primary text | #ededed | #efe9de | #eef0f5 |\n| Secondary text | #a1a1a6 | #aaa398 | #a3a9b8 |\n| Muted text and icons | #85858b | #8a847a | #7a8296 |\n\n### Light\n\n| Role | Graphite | Warm | Ink |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Page background | #fafafa | #f7f4ee | #f5f7fa |\n| Card | #ffffff | #fdfbf7 | #ffffff |\n| Raised, like an active row or input | #f4f4f5 | #efe9df | #eceff4 |\n| Hairline | primary text at 8% | primary text at 8% | primary text at 8% |\n| Primary text | #171717 | #1f1b16 | #111827 |\n| Secondary text | #525252 | #5e554a | #4b5565 |\n| Muted text and icons | #737373 | #7b7266 | #6b7487 |\n\nMuted text is for meta and captions only and stays near 4.5 to 1 on its surface. Body tex\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] The direction is written and every pass follows it\n- [ ] Every spacing value is on the 4px scale and equal things have equal sizes\n- [ ] Icons, labels and numbers each sit in one aligned column\n- [ ] One tinted neutral ramp, three text roles, one accent with enough contrast\n- [ ] No hue gradients, gradient text, glows, colored shadows or color blobs\n- [ ] No emoji left in the interface\n- [ ] One icon set at one size and stroke\n- [ ] Type chosen for the character, weights 400 to 600, no uppercase tracked labels, no italic, no negative tracking on small text\n- [ ] Right to left screens follow [references/rtl.md](references/rtl.md): `dir` and `lang` set, logical properties, the layout mirrored, directional icons flipped, mixed text isolated, one digit system, zero letter spacing on Arabic\n- [ ] No exclamation marks, no trailing periods on short strings, no greeting headline, no unverified claims\n- [ ] Numbers in one format with tabular figures\n- [ ] Nested radii add up\n- [ ] Table row states read apart: header, value in use, selection and keyboard focus are each recognizable\n- [ ] Buttons share the control radius, no square corners against the component style\n- [ ] A hero has a real visual or a product preview with at least one moment, never filler and never a labeled placeholder\n- [ ] The first screen is composed as a whole with no dead empty band\n- [ ] No looping or bouncy motion, one easing curve, no animated layout properties\n- [ ] Template check. If you swapped the product name for another product's, would the screen still fit? If yes, push the direction harder in the copy, the visual and the signature detail", "files": []}, {"id": "builtin-material-design-3-ui", "name": "material-design-3-ui", "desc": "Chuẩn Google Material Design 3, Material You & M3 Expressive motion", "builtin": true, "prompt": "# Material Design 3 UI/UX\n\n## Purpose\n\nCreate interfaces that **behave like Material Design 3**, not interfaces that merely look rounded or “Google-like.”\n\nTreat M3 as a system:\n\n**user goal → information architecture → hierarchy → adaptive layout → semantic tokens → components → states → interaction → motion → accessibility → visual expression**\n\nM3 Expressive is optional. Use it to strengthen hierarchy, usability, recognition, and emotional clarity; never use it as permission to make every element loud.\n\n## Source of truth\n\n1.  Prefer current official Material 3 guidance at `https://m3.material.io/`.\n2.  For Android implementation details, prefer `https://developer.android.com/`.\n3.  Treat design guidance separately from implementation-library/API status.\n4.  Verify current official guidance before asserting exact dimensions, dependency versions, experimental API names, or recently changed behavior.\n5.  Do not silently mix Material 2 rules into Material 3.\n6.  Pixel and Google-app screenshots are examples, not the specification.\n\n## Core rules\n\n### MUST\n\n- Start from the user’s task, content, hierarchy, and platform before styling.\n- Use semantic design tokens instead of scattered one-off values.\n- Choose components by purpose and behavior, not visual resemblance.\n- Prefer established M3/platform components when they solve the interaction.\n- Define important states: default, pressed, focus, hover where applicable, selected, disabled, loading, error, empty, and success where relevant.\n- Design for the available window rather than a guessed device category.\n- Preserve accessible contrast, target size, focus, semantics, scaling, and non-color state communication.\n- Respect system bars, safe areas, cutouts, keyboards/IME, foldable hinges, and edge-to-edge behavior where applicable.\n- Keep primary actions distinguishable from secondary and destructive actions.\n- Make custom components inherit the token, state, motion, and accessibility logic of the system.\n- Explain deliberate departures from M3 when product requirements justify them.\n\n### MUST NOT\n\n- Do not “Materialize” a screen by only adding large radii, pastel colors, shadows, or Material Symbols.\n- Do not hardcode raw colors throughout screens when semantic roles can be used.\n- Do not use color alone to communicate meaning or state.\n- Do not wrap every section or list row in a card.\n- Do not maximize corner radius on every component.\n- Do not use FABs for minor, destructive, or ambiguous actions.\n- Do not use chips as generic buttons or primary navigation.\n- Do not use tabs for unrelated top-level destinations.\n- Do not make all components equally expressive.\n- Do not invent a new control when an established M3/platform control already solves the interaction.\n- Do not sacrifice accessibility to preserve a visual composition.\n- Do not stretch a compact/mobile layout unchanged across wide windows.\n\n## Progressive-disclosure references\n\nLoad only the references needed for the current task. Do not read every reference by default.\n\n| Need                                                    | Read                                  |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| Color roles, surfaces, dark theme, dynamic color        | `references/color-system.md`          |\n| Type hierarchy, scaling, expressive typography          | `references/typography.md`            |\n| Shape, cards, containment, elevation                    | `references/shape-and-elevation.md`   |\n| Spacing, alignment, readable widths, safe areas         | `references/spacing-and-layout.md`    |\n| Buttons, FAB, cards, chips, selection, inputs, feedback | `references/component-selection.md`   |\n| Navigation bar/rail/drawer, tabs, app bars              | `references/navigation.md`            |\n| Forms, validation, settings, text input                 | `references/forms-and-input.md`       |\n| Snackbar, dialog, sheet, loading, empty/error states    | `references/feedback-and-overlays.md` |\n| Responsive, tablet, foldable, desktop, multi-pane       | `references/adaptive-design.md`       |\n| Accessibility audit or any final UI review              | `references/accessibility.md`         |\n| Transitions, feedback animation, shape morphing         | `references/motion.md`                |\n| Material 3 Expressive                                   | `references/m3-expressive.md`         |\n| Final audit / suspicious “Material-looking” UI          | `references/anti-patterns.md`         |\n\nFor a broad end-to-end design, load the references incrementally as decisions require them. Always include the accessibility reference before final approval.\n\n## Workflow\n\nFollow this order unless the task explicitly scopes one stage.\n\n### 1. Understand the product\n\nIdentify the platform, primary user goal and action, top-level destinations, content hierarchy, data density, input methods, brand constraints, required states and edge cases, target window sizes, and whether M3 Expressive is appropriate.\n\nIf missing information does not block the work, make a conservative M3-aligned assumption and state it. Ask only when the missing information materially changes architecture or interaction.\n\n### 2. Establish information architecture\n\nBefore styling:\n\n- Group related information and identify the primary task per screen.\n- Separate navigation from actions and distinguish destructive actions.\n- Remove duplicated controls and define progressive disclosure.\n- Avoid showing information merely because space is available.\n\n### 3. Choose adaptive structure\n\nRead `references/adaptive-design.md` and `references/navigation.md` when multiple window sizes or navigation forms matter.\n\nPrefer canonical patterns such as list-detail, supporting pane, feed, and adaptive navigation when they fit the content.\n\nWider space must improve context or productivity rather than merely stretch content.\n\n### 4. Build the theme semantically\n\nRead the relevant color, typography, shape/elevation, and spacing references.\n\nUse the hierarchy:\n\n**reference/system tokens → semantic/system roles → component tokens**\n\nScreens should depend on semantic roles rather than scattered literal values.\n\n### 5. Select components\n\nRead `references/component-selection.md` plus specialized navigation/form/feedback references as needed.\n\nFor each important control, determine its semantic purpose, emphasis, immediate or transactional behavior, interaction states, accessibility behavior, and adaptive behavior.\n\n### 6. Define states and feedback\n\nCover relevant loading, empty, error, success, disabled, selected, pressed, focus, hover, and busy/submitting states.\n\nError states must provide a recovery path.\n\n### 7. Apply accessibility\n\nRead `references/accessibility.md`.\n\nAccessibility is a release requirement. Do not defer it to visual polish.\n\n### 8. Add motion\n\nRead `references/motion.md` only when motion is part of the task.\n\nMotion must explain state, hierarchy, spatial relationship, or response. Do not animate for spectacle.\n\n### 9. Apply M3 Expressive if appropriate\n\nRead `references/m3-expressive.md`.\n\nUse a few deliberate expressive moments. Routine reading, forms, settings, and dense productivity surfaces should remain calm unless stronger expression improves usability.\n\n### 10. Audit before approval\n\nRead `references/anti-patterns.md` and run the self-audit below.\n\n## Self-audit\n\nBefore delivering a design, score each applicable category `0`, `1`, or `2`.\n\n- `0` = incorrect / missing\n- `1` = partially correct / needs refinement\n- `2` = ready\n\n| Category              | Check                                                        |\n|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Task clarity          | Primary user goal and action are obvious                     |\n| Information hierarchy | Grouping and emphasis are coherent                           |\n| Component semantics   | Controls match their actual behavior                         |\n| Token discipline      | Semantic roles are used consistently                         |\n| Adaptive behavior     | Layout improves across relevant windows                      |\n| States & feedback     | Important states and recovery are covered                    |\n| Accessibility         | Targets, contrast, semantics, focus, scaling, reduced motion |\n| Expressive restraint  | Expression improves hierarchy without creating noise         |\n\nA design with any `0` in \n\n### M3 Expressive\n# Material 3 Expressive\n\nUse this reference only when M3 Expressive is requested or clearly appropriate.\n\nM3 Expressive extends Material 3. It is not a separate excuse to ignore M3 semantics.\n\n## Expressive levers\n\n- color\n- shape\n- size\n- motion\n- containment\n- typography\n\n## Rules\n\n- Use expression to improve hierarchy, usability, recognition, or emotional clarity.\n- Make important actions easier to find, not merely more decorative.\n- Use size contrast to establish priority.\n- Use shape contrast to distinguish meaningful roles or states.\n- Use vibrant color selectively.\n- Use containment to clarify groups and isolate key actions.\n- Use motion to explain state and spatial relationships.\n- Use emphasized typography selectively.\n- Prefer a few strong expressive moments over uniform intensity.\n- Preserve familiarity: buttons must still read as buttons, navigation as navigation, and selection as selection.\n- Keep routine forms, settings, reading, and dense productivity surfaces calmer unless stronger expression measurably improves scanning.\n\n## Candidate expressive patterns\n\nDepending on current platform support: - button groups, - split buttons, - floating toolbars, - expressive list items, - richer shape families, - emphasized typography, - updated loading/progress experiences, - shape-morphing interactions.\n\nVerify implementation-library support before naming an exact API.\n\n## Restraint test\n\nAsk: 1. What deserves the user’s attention first? 2. Which one or two expressive levers best reinforce that priority? 3. Can routine content remain calmer? 4. Does the UI remain unders\n\n### M3 Motion\n# Motion\n\nUse this reference when specifying transitions, feedback, shape morphing, or expressive animation.\n\n## Purpose\n\nMotion must communicate.\n\nUse it to: - connect origin and destination, - explain hierarchy changes, - acknowledge input, - reveal or hide content, - preserve continuity during navigation, - preserve continuity during adaptive layout changes.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Prefer Material motion schemes/tokens when available.\n- Keep repeated utility interactions quick and unobtrusive.\n- Reserve stronger spring or shape motion for moments that benefit from it.\n- Do not animate everything.\n- Do not delay task completion for spectacle.\n- Do not make motion the only explanation of state.\n- Respect reduced-motion preferences.\n- Keep spatial transitions consistent with the interface’s navigation model.\n\n## M3 Expressive motion\n\nExpressive motion may add personality through spring behavior, shape changes, or stronger transitions, but it should still explain: - what changed, - where content came from, - what the user’s action affected.\n\nIf removing the animation makes the interaction incomprehensible, the static states are under-designed.\n\n## Review questions\n\n- What information does this animation communicate?\n- Is the duration proportional to frequency and importance?\n- Does reduced m\n\n### Anti-Patterns\n# Material 3 Anti-Patterns\n\nUse this reference during audits and before final approval.\n\nReject or revise designs with these patterns unless a strong product requirement justifies them.\n\n## Fake-Material styling\n\n- Every section is inside a rounded card.\n- Every control is a pill/capsule.\n- Arbitrary gradients are used as a shortcut to “Material.”\n- Default-looking Material purple is applied everywhere without semantic reasoning.\n- Shadows are added to every container.\n- Material Symbols are treated as sufficient proof of M3 compliance.\n\n## Component misuse\n\n- Multiple competing FABs.\n- Chips used as generic buttons.\n- Chips used as primary navigation.\n- Tabs used for unrelated top-level destinations.\n- Multiple visually dominant filled buttons in one immediate action group.\n- A switch plus redundant Save button for a simple immediate setting.\n- Custom controls recreate established platform/M3 behavior without a reason.\n- Destructive and routine primary actions are styled identically.\n\n## Layout problems\n\n- Every list row becomes a card.\n- Cards are nested repeatedly.\n- A fixed mobile canvas is centered unchanged on desktop.\n- Content stretches to huge line lengths on wide screens.\n- Navigation destination order changes unpredictably between breakpoints.\n- Essential actions disappear during adaptive navigation changes.\n- Safe areas, IME, or foldable hinges are ignored.\n\n## Accessibility failures\n\n- Tiny icon-only controls.\n- Low-contrast gray-on-gray text.\n- Color is the only state indicator.\n- Focus is invisible or illogical.\n- Custom controls lack semantics/keyboard behav", "files": []}];
